    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson.jpg" alt="Thomas Jefferson" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Official_Presidential_portrait_of_Thomas_Jefferson_(by_Rembrandt_Peale,_1800).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rembrandt Peale, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> grew out of thirteen British colonies along the Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, founded between the settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamestown">Jamestown</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> in 1607 and the chartering of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> in 1732. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>'s victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>, Parliament sought to tax the colonies to help pay for their defence, through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stamp-act-1765">Stamp Act</a> of 1765 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/townshend-acts">Townshend duties</a> of 1767, provoking protests against taxation without representation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston-tea-party">Boston Tea Party</a> of 1773 led to the punitive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/intolerable-acts">Coercive Acts</a>, and the colonies coordinated their resistance through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-continental-congress">First Continental Congress</a> in 1774. Fighting began at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battles-of-lexington-and-concord">Lexington and Concord</a> in April 1775. On 4 July 1776 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-continental-congress">Second Continental Congress</a> adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-declaration-of-independence">Declaration of Independence</a>, drafted mainly by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-jefferson">Thomas Jefferson</a>, which justified separation by appeal to natural rights. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/articles-of-confederation">Articles of Confederation</a>, in force from 1781, created a loose league of sovereign states with a Congress unable to levy taxes. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1783">Treaty of Paris</a> of 1783 recognised independence, with a western border on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, debts and unrest such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shays-rebellion">Shays' Rebellion</a> of 1786–1787 persuaded leaders to call the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-convention-united-states">Constitutional Convention</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amman">Philadelphia</a> in 1787. The Constitution it drafted was ratified in 1788 and took effect in 1789, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a> as the first president.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/erie-canal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/erie-canal.jpg" alt="Erie Canal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/erie-canal">Erie Canal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sunset_over_the_Erie_Canal_in_North_Tonawanda,_NY..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Joshua Karn, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The early republic was overwhelmingly agricultural. In the North family farms, shipping and, from the 1790s, textile mills in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a> dominated, while in the South plantations worked by enslaved people of African descent produced tobacco, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> and above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, whose output soared after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eli-whitney">Eli Whitney</a>'s cotton gin of 1793; by 1860 nearly four million people were enslaved. Canals such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erie-canal">Erie Canal</a>, opened in 1825, and then railways bound the interior to the eastern ports, and the first transcontinental railroad was completed in 1869. After the Civil War industrialisation, built on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>, oil, railways and electricity, made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> the largest economy in the world by the late nineteenth century, while more than 20 million immigrants arrived, mainly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, between 1880 and 1920. Religion was diverse and voluntary, with no established national church under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-amendment-to-the-united-states-constitution">First Amendment</a>, and waves of Protestant revival inspired reform movements from abolition to temperance. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-depression">Great Depression</a> that followed the crash of 1929 left about a quarter of the workforce unemployed and brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-deal">New Deal</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franklin-d-roosevelt">Franklin D. Roosevelt</a>. After 1945 the country produced a large share of world output, suburban mass consumption spread, and the dollar became the world's main reserve currency. In recent decades services and technology have replaced manufacturing as the core of the economy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battles-of-saratoga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battles-of-saratoga.jpg" alt="Battles of Saratoga" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battles-of-saratoga">Battles of Saratoga</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Surrender_of_General_Burgoyne.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">John Trumbull, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Continental Army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-washington">George Washington</a>, aided from 1778 by an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, won independence despite repeated defeats; the surrender of British armies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battles-of-saratoga">Saratoga</a> in 1777 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-of-yorktown">Yorktown</a> in 1781 proved decisive. The young republic kept only a small regular army and relied on state militias, but it fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> again in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a> and waged a long series of wars against Native American nations that drove them west and onto reservations. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a> of 1846–1848 added <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a> and the Southwest. The Civil War of 1861–1865, the deadliest conflict in American history, with between about 620,000 and 750,000 soldiers dead, ended in Union victory over the Confederacy after battles such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gettysburg">Gettysburg</a> in 1863. Victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1898 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, Guam and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a>. American troops helped tip the balance on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-front-world-war-i">Western Front</a> in 1918, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> entered after the Japanese attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attack-on-pearl-harbor">Pearl Harbor</a> on 7 December 1941, its industry and forces fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and the Pacific, and the war ended after atomic bombs were dropped on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> it led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>, founded in 1949, fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a> and built a global network of bases; since then it has fought in the Gulf, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> and remains the world's strongest military power.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/united-states-bill-of-rights"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/united-states-bill-of-rights.jpg" alt="United States Bill of Rights" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/united-states-bill-of-rights">United States Bill of Rights</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bill_of_Rights_-_DPLA_-_a65f6d0b6210c18d17c596908ebcf270_(page_1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Archives and Records Administration. Office of the Federal Register. 4/1/1985, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Constitution of 1787 created a federal republic in which power was divided between the national government and the states and, at the national level, separated among Congress, a president chosen through an electoral college, and a judiciary headed by the Supreme Court. Its first ten amendments, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-bill-of-rights">Bill of Rights</a> of 1791, protected freedom of speech, religion and the press and the rights of the accused. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marbury-v-madison">Marbury v. Madison</a> in 1803 the Supreme Court under Chief Justice <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-marshall">John Marshall</a> asserted the power of judicial review. Party politics emerged early, with Federalists against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republican-party">Jeffersonian Republicans</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-united-states">Democrats</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/whig-party-united-states">Whigs</a> and, from the 1850s, Democrats against the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/republican-party-united-states">Republican Party</a>. The franchise widened from property-owning white men to almost all white men by the 1830s. The Reconstruction amendments abolished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in 1865 and promised equal protection and the vote to Black men, but Southern states soon disenfranchised them and imposed segregation through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jim-crow-laws">Jim Crow laws</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineteenth-amendment-to-the-united-states-constitution">Nineteenth Amendment</a> gave women the vote in 1920. Federal power grew sharply with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-deal">New Deal</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, creating Social Security, regulatory agencies and a large national security establishment. The civil rights movement, with leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther-king-jr">Martin Luther King Jr.</a>, won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-rights-act-of-1964">Civil Rights Act of 1964</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voting-rights-act-of-1965">Voting Rights Act of 1965</a>, ending legal segregation and restoring Black voting rights in the South.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/fourteen-points"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fourteen-points.jpg" alt="Fourteen Points" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/fourteen-points">Fourteen Points</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:William_Orpen_Woodrow_Wilson_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">William Orpen, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> was the first modern state founded on a written declaration of natural rights and a written national constitution, and both became models: the Declaration influenced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> and later independence movements, while federalism, presidential government and judicial review were copied across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> and beyond. Its westward expansion displaced and devastated Native American nations and, through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexican-american-war">Mexican–American War</a>, took about half of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s territory. As a destination for tens of millions of immigrants it became a nation of unusually diverse origins. In the twentieth century its power shaped the world order: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woodrow-wilson">Woodrow Wilson</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourteen-points">Fourteen Points</a> of 1918 promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/self-determination">national self-determination</a>, and after 1945 the United States led the creation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-monetary-fund">International Monetary Fund</a> and the World Bank, and financed European recovery through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-plan">Marshall Plan</a>. Its rivalry with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, including the space race that landed astronauts on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhu">Moon</a> in 1969, defined world politics for four decades. American inventors, universities and companies drove much of modern technology, from electric light and powered flight to the transistor and the internet, and its films, music and consumer culture spread worldwide. Its record also includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, segregation and contested interventions abroad, which remain central to debates about its legacy.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/new-deal"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-deal.jpg" alt="New Deal" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/new-deal">New Deal</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:New_Deal_collage.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">LordHarris at English Wikipedia, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> has never ceased to exist, but it has been transformed several times. The Civil War replaced a union of states in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> was protected with a stronger nation in which it was abolished, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-deal">New Deal</a> and the world wars turned a limited federal government into a modern administrative and military state. After victory in 1945 the United States became one of two superpowers, and with the dissolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1991 it remained the only one. Since then it has faced the terrorist attacks of 11 September 2001, long wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> from 2001 to 2021 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> from 2003, the financial crisis of 2008 and the rise of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> as an economic and strategic rival, while deep political polarisation has tested its institutions. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alaska">Alaska</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> became the 49th and 50th states in 1959, and the country also holds territories including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> and Guam. With about 340 million people it is the third most populous country in the world, and it remains the largest economy by nominal output, the issuer of the main reserve currency and the leading military power: a federal republic of 50 states governed under the Constitution of 1787, the oldest written national constitution still in force.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, founded on a written declaration of rights and the oldest national constitution still in force, grew from thirteen colonies into a continental federal republic and, after victory in the two world wars, into the dominant economic, military and cultural power of the modern world.</em></p>

